WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.120
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:12.400
I have to say we haven’t had a simulation paper in&nbsp;
a while so today’s episode is going to be my way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:16.880
of medicating myself. You are more&nbsp;
than welcome to watch the process.

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:24.080
And this paper is about performing collision&nbsp;
detection. You see, when we write a simple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:30.240
space game, detecting whether a collision has&nbsp;
happened or not is a mostly trivial endeavor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:37.040
However, now, instead, let’s look&nbsp;
at the kind of simulation complexity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:43.520
that you are expecting from a Two Minute Papers&nbsp;
video…first, let’s try to screw this bolt in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:52.800
using an industry standard simulation&nbsp;
system, and it is…stuck. Hm…why?

00:00:53.680 --> 00:01:00.080
Because here, we would need to simulate in&nbsp;
detail, not only whether two things collide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:08.160
they collide all the time, but, we need to check&nbsp;
for and simulate friction too! Let’s see what this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:16.240
new simulation method does with the same scene.&nbsp;
And…oh yes! This one isn’t screwing with us and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:23.680
does the job perfectly. Excellent. However, this&nbsp;
was not nearly the most complex thing it can do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:29.840
Let’s try some crazy geometry, with&nbsp;
crazy movements and tons of friction.

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:37.360
There we go. This one will do. Welcome&nbsp;
to the expanding lock box experiment. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:45.280
what is this? Look, as we turn the key,&nbsp;
the locking pins retract, and the bottom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:52.480
is now allowed to fall. This scene contains&nbsp;
tens to hundreds of thousands of contacts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:59.840
and yet, it still works perfectly. Beautiful.&nbsp;
I love this one because with this simulation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:06.640
we can test intricate mechanisms for robotics and&nbsp;
more before committing to manufacturing anything.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:13.760
And, unlike with previous methods, we don’t need&nbsp;
to worry whether the simulation is correct or not,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:21.920
and we can be sure that if we 3D print this,&nbsp;
it will behave exactly this way. So good!

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:26.160
Also, here come some of my favorite&nbsp;
experiments from the paper.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:32.840
For instance, it can also simulate a&nbsp;
piston attached to a rotating disk, or,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:38.720
smooth motion on one wheel, leading to&nbsp;
intermittent motion on the other one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:46.560
And, if you feel the urge to build a&nbsp;
virtual bike, do not worry for a second,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:53.200
because your chain and sprocket mechanisms&nbsp;
will work exactly as you expect to. Loving it.

00:02:54.160 --> 00:03:01.680
Now, interestingly, look here. The time step size&nbsp;
used with the new technique is a hundred times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:07.440
bigger, which is great, we can advance the time&nbsp;
in bigger pieces when computing the simulation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:16.160
That is good news indeed. However, every time we&nbsp;
do so, we still have to compute a great deal more.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:22.240
The resulting computation time is still at least&nbsp;
a hundred times slower than previous methods.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:29.120
However, those methods don’t count, at least&nbsp;
not on these scenes, because they have produced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:35.680
incorrect results. Look at it some other way -&nbsp;
this is the fastest simulator that actually works.

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:41.280
Still, it is not that slow. The one&nbsp;
with the intermittent motion takes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:46.160
less than a second per time step, which&nbsp;
likely means a few seconds per frame,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:53.440
while the bolt screwing scene is likely in the&nbsp;
minutes per frame domain. Very impressive! And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:58.560
if you are a seasoned Fellow Scholar, you&nbsp;
know what’s coming, this is where we invoke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:04.480
the First Law Of Papers, which says that research&nbsp;
is a process. Do not look at where we are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:12.080
look at where we will be two more papers down the&nbsp;
line. And two more papers down the line, I am sure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:18.960
even the more complex simulations will be done&nbsp;
in a matter of seconds. What a time to be alive!

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:17.360
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

